+// Week 6, Problem 2: priority scheduler.
+//
+// Instead of running every RUNNABLE process in plain proc[] order,
+// each pass now does two scans:
+//   1. Find the smallest priority value among RUNNABLE processes
+//      (0 = highest priority, 63 = lowest), touching one p->lock at
+//      a time and releasing it before moving to the next slot --
+//      never holding two proc locks at once, exactly like the
+//      original loop and exactly like getprocstatebypid()/kill().
+//   2. Scan proc[] again and run every still-RUNNABLE process with
+//      that best priority value. This preserves stock xv6's
+//      round-robin-ish behavior among equal-priority processes while
+//      still preventing lower-priority processes from running while a
+//      higher-priority process is runnable. Each candidate is
+//      re-checked under its own lock before switching, because it may
+//      have changed state between the two scans.
+//
+// Tie-break policy (documented per the assignment): among processes
+// tied for the lowest priority value, scan 2 runs them in proc[]
+// order, just like stock xv6's scheduler loop. This usually, but not
+// necessarily always, tracks PID order -- proc[] slots get reused as
+// processes exit, so slot order and PID order can drift apart on a
+// long-running system. I chose this over a strict lowest-PID-only
+// tie-break so equal-priority CPU-bound processes still take turns.
 void
 scheduler(void)
 {
