+// Week 6, Problem 3: expose whether cons.lock is currently held.
+//
+// The assignment describes "extern declare cons.lock in sysproc.c,"
+// but that doesn't actually work cleanly in C: `cons` here is an
+// instance of an ANONYMOUS struct type (no tag name), so another
+// file has no type it could use to declare a matching `extern`
+// variable and then reach into its .lock field -- you'd have to
+// duplicate this exact, otherwise-private struct layout (including
+// the input buffer) into sysproc.c just to read one field. A small
+// accessor function is the standard, robust fix: it keeps `cons`'s
+// layout private to this file, and is exactly the same idea as
+// getprocstatebypid() exposing state from proc[] without exposing
+// proc[] itself.
+int
+cons_lock_held(void)
+{
+  return cons.lock.locked;
+}
